t who  treats pretty much everyone like dogs  stella come has got skeletons as well as  couture in her closet  and as characters that represent a clash  between the old fashioned establishment  and the cutting-edge future thompson and  stone play off each other to truly  wicked effect  1965 collection oh no wonder i love it  it's mine  i fixed it the three dalmatians owned by  the baroness in this movie are a world  away from the sweet and friendly ones in  both the 1961 and 1996 films  as the baroness herself says they're  gorgeous and vicious  it's my favorite combination and oh boy  she's not kidding  it's hardly surprising these dogs act so  vicious though  after all they do belong to the baroness  and she's viciousness personified  the dalmatians appear in several crucial  scenes that point at why corella feels  the way she does about the breed in the  original films  but unlike those movies this isn't their  story so expect to see  less of them here however cruella and  friends both have their own adorably  scrappy pooches buddy and wink that join  them on their adventures so there's  still plenty of canine shenanigans to  enjoy throughout the story  in a change from the animation but just  like the live-action remake there's no  talking animals here and the animals you  do see on screen are a mix of real-life  pups and cgi  which is similar to the 90s film though  that movie also used puppets in a few  scenes  in contrast to the 60s animation and 90s  flick which were both set around the  time they were made  cruella's new origin story issues a  contemporary setting  opting instead for the 1970s which is  the perfect backdrop for her  anarchic attitude the culture clash of  that particular period plays out  beautifully in the contrasting styles of  the baroness and cruella  the baroness has shades of establishment  fashion houses like dior about her  versus cruella's more punk sensibilities  of vivian westwood  but this isn't the first time corella's  worked 